63. Unique Paths II (DP with Obstacles)

🧠 Problem
Given an m x n grid:

Start at (0,0)
Reach (m-1, n-1)
Moves allowed:
Right →
Down ↓
Some cells have obstacles (1) → cannot pass

👉 Find total unique paths avoiding obstacles

🔑 Core Idea (MOST IMPORTANT)

👉 Think backward

At cell (i, j):

“Where did I come from?”

From top → (i-1, j)
From left → (i, j-1)
❗ Twist (Obstacle)

👉 If current cell is obstacle:

dp[i][j] = 0

👉 No path can go through it

🔁 Recurrence

dp[i][j]={0                      if obstacle
          dp[i−1][j]+dp[i][j−1]  otherwise​
}


🧠 State Definition
dp[i][j] = number of ways to reach cell (i, j)
🧩 Base Case
dp[0][0] = 1 (only if NOT obstacle)

👉 If (0,0) is obstacle → answer = 0

⚠️ Boundary Handling
if i > 0 → can come from top
if j > 0 → can come from left
🧠 Intuition (VERY IMPORTANT)

👉 We do NOT “avoid obstacles manually”

Instead:

Obstacle cell → contributes 0
Valid cells → sum of valid paths
🔍 Example Thinking

If:

top = obstacle → dp[i-1][j] = 0
left = valid   → dp[i][j-1] = x

👉 Then:

dp[i][j] = 0 + x = x

👉 Automatically ignores obstacle

⚙️ DP Construction (Bottom-Up)
class Solution {
    public int uniquePathsWithObstacles(int[][] obstacleGrid) {
        int m = obstacleGrid.length;
        int n = obstacleGrid[0].length;

        int[][] dp = new int[m][n];

        for(int i = 0; i < m; i++){
            for(int j = 0; j < n; j++){

                if(obstacleGrid[i][j] == 1){
                    dp[i][j] = 0;
                    continue;
                }

                if(i == 0 && j == 0){
                    dp[i][j] = 1;
                    continue;
                }

                int up = 0, left = 0;

                if(i > 0) up = dp[i-1][j];
                if(j > 0) left = dp[i][j-1];

                dp[i][j] = up + left;
            }
        }

        return dp[m-1][n-1];
    }
}
